    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c2917a73_f803a364",
        "filename": "core/java/android/os/Parcel.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 3371,
      "author": {
        "id": 1133744
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-26T17:46:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Interesting case! \n\nIn the bundle case we already extract the part responsible for the bundle out of the parcel http://cs/android/frameworks/base/core/java/android/os/BaseBundle.java?l\u003d1675-1683\u0026rcl\u003d8ab91d385c451d9a24e5aa115587ae6582674807, so any length maliciously put here can\u0027t affect the parcel used to produce the bundle in the first place. In my tests an overlength results in an extra null \u003d\u003e null in the map since all the next key-value reads will produce null \u003d\u003e null.\n\nIf you\u0027re worried about it in the context of Parcel APIs themselves, you can\u0027t really trust serialized data coming from untrusted sources. For example, if you\u0027re putting an app-produced data in the same parcel as trusted data then passing that onto another component, then the trusted part could already be tainted by using a bundle (oh, the irony) and controlling its length written on the wire to make the reader position the cursor where you want.\n\nI changed the code to use MathUtils.addOrThrow() like bundle.\n\nLet me know your thoughts!",
      "parentUuid": "d70a3997_04fdd10b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3371,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 3371,
